# scp — real-world examples

## Local -> remote

```sh
scp foo.txt user@example.com:remote/dir
```

## Remote -> local

```sh
scp user@example.com:remote/dir/foo.txt local/dir
```

## Common switches

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-i key.pem` | Identity file (ssh-key auth) |
| `-r` | Recursively copy directories |
| `-v` | Verbose / debugging output |
| `-p` | Preserve modification times and modes |
| `-C` | Compress in transit (skip for already-compressed data like images/video) |
| `-P 4000` | Remote SSH port (capital P — lowercase -p means preserve) |

## Worked example

```sh
# Copy from server2's Downloads: recursive, compress, preserve times/modes, verbose,
# using a key and a nonstandard port, into the current directory
scp -i server2.pem -P 3003 -Cvpr user2@server2:/home/user2/Downloads .
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Push a config to homelab on its custom ssh port
scp -P 2222 ~/configs/smb.conf david@homelab:/tmp/smb.conf

# Pull a Plex log bundle off mediaserver
scp -r david@mediaserver:'/var/lib/plexmediaserver/.../Logs' ~/plex-logs/

# Copy a large ISO WITHOUT -C (already compressed; compression wastes CPU)
scp ~/iso/openSUSE-Tumbleweed.iso david@homelab:/srv/iso/
```

> Note: on newer OpenSSH, `scp` uses the SFTP protocol under the hood, and many
> guides now recommend `rsync` or `sftp` for large or repeated transfers.
